  "selected": [
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "Bias, RMSE and pattern correlation against WOA23 must use one grid and one mask over all three models and WOA23, and the WOA23 land-sea mask must be applied consistently for Figure 1.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The bias, RMSE and pattern correlation against WOA23 must come from one sample: regrid the three models and WOA23 to the 1° grid, build one mask with `build_comparison_mask` over all of them, apply it with `.where(mask)`, and rank the models only on it. Report the cell counts the mask records. The reference is WOA23, so the default `ocean_only=True` is right.",
        "Report `mean_data`, `mean_reference` and the bias from `compute_difference_metrics` over the shared sample. State that these means are not the global means of each source over its own ocean, which are a different quantity. Do not apply this shared mask to trends or to ΔSSS."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "historical-ssp245-member-continuity",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/historical-ssp245-member-continuity.md",
      "sha256": "02341c56015c6296db14585b4561fc32ca6b4e52e2264bec50eee6746c1d55f6",
      "reason": "CESM2 historical (r1i1p1f1) and ssp245 (r10i1p1f1) are different members, so delta SSS and the slope ratio mix forced change with internal variability and the answer must state this caveat.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "CESM2 historical is r1i1p1f1 but its ssp245 is r10i1p1f1, whose parent is historical r10, and r10 is absent locally. Its ΔSSS (ssp245 2071-2100 minus historical 1995-2014) therefore includes forced change plus the difference in internal variability between two members. State this caveat for CESM2.",
        "GFDL-ESM4 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR use r1 for both experiments, so their join is continuous. The ssp245 and historical slopes are fitted separately and not across 2014/2015, so the ratio of the slopes is not a continuous-trajectory quantity. For CESM2 never fit or draw across the join."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "Delta SSS is defined relative to the 1995-2014 historical baseline, and the bias is against an observed 1971-2000 climatology, so the baseline periods must be stated and kept distinct.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The model climatology (1971-2000) and the WOA23 normal (1971-2000) are the baselines. State them, and note that the bias keeps each model's mean-state error.",
        "The trends use annual means of each model's own series. These are not anomalies against WOA23."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "basin-and-region-definitions",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/basin-and-region-definitions.md",
      "sha256": "d0d90c32fab9f6e0a39d4f769a4e902e6412b2ad1b09d9c7d19f6bb3aa426611",
      "reason": "Basin means for Atlantic, Indian and Pacific depend on the partition used (WOA23 basinmask_01, marginal seas folded in, Southern and Arctic excluded) and the answer must say so.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Use the WOA23 `basinmask_01` via `select_basin`, with `include_marginal_seas=True` (marginal seas folded into the parent ocean). Southern and Arctic are separate basins and are excluded. State this mask and these choices.",
        "Apply the same basin mask to the models and to the observations. Use the surface labels for SSS, since the 0-10 m layer is shallow. Marginal seas missing from a model have no counterpart."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "The global means need correct weighting (volume weights per the question's definition, area for the surface) over all ocean cells, without subsampling.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The global mean SSS is a weighted mean over valid cells only (NaN-aware weights). Use `compute_basic_stat` with `op='area_weighted_mean'`, or the volume-weighted form for the 0-10 m levels, where each level's weight is its full cell volume. Plain `op='mean'` is wrong.",
        "RMSE, bias and pattern correlation need area weighting: `compute_difference_metrics` does this by default, and `analyze_correlation` needs `weighting='area'`. Basin and global ΔSSS means use the same weights, restricted to valid cells."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-static-grid-fields",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-static-grid-fields.md",
      "sha256": "2ad0548ca0993fa57d8f1fad27a267dcf9c0e1d007a74343d4bbdbf5b4f1ca17",
      "reason": "Each model's areacello and volcello supply the cell-volume weights, and the full-cell volume weighting of the 0-10 m levels depends on them.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Layer thickness is `volcello` / `areacello`, not the `lev_bnds` difference, which fails at partial cells (GFDL-ESM4 is off by 14 % in cells). Use each model's own `areacello` and `volcello`. Their origin is recorded in `attrs['area_source']` and `attrs['volume_source']`, so report it.",
        "GFDL-ESM4's `volcello` varies in time, so handle the time axis. Do not rescale total ocean areas to match between models."
      ]
    },
    {
